<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Saldaña</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1030 CE (approx.) · capital Saldaña</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The lordship of the Banu Gómez family on the upper Carrión, powerful nobles of León who sometimes allied with Almanzor.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the late 9th and early 10th centuries the Leonese kings granted the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saldana">Saldaña</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrion-de-los-condes">Carrión</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liebana">Liébana</a> on the upper Carrión river to Count Diego Muñoz, founder of the family later called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-gomez">Banu Gómez</a> in Arabic sources. The county was part of the resettlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> plain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts were among the most powerful nobles of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-leon">Kingdom of León</a>, ruling large estates and castles and often rebelling against the crown. Count García Gómez allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> and took part in his campaigns in the 990s. The county's towns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saldana">Saldaña</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrion-de-los-condes">Carrión</a>, lay on the route that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-de-santiago">Way of St James</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-gomez">Banu Gómez</a> in the early 11th century, the county passed into the domains of the kings of León and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrion-de-los-condes">Carrión</a> later belonged to the Ansúrez family, and its counts appear in the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 900 CE – 1030 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:77.61%;width:2.59%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1030 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saldaña</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Diego Muñoz, García Gómez</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banu Gómez counties, County of Carrión</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=965&amp;focus=county-of-saldana" aria-label="County of Saldaña on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4565 666 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Saldaña lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4885 922L4865 919"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4865 919h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4885 922h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4885 922h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Saldaña. <a href="/atlas?year=965&amp;focus=county-of-saldana">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
